^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG: this exposes a genuine std::span constructor bug, and the test was
previously an UNSOUND vacuous pass.

Before on-demand iterator member-function instantiation, instantiating
std::span<int> failed to complete its iterator type (the C++20 member alias
`iterator_concept = __iter_concept<_Iterator>` threw while the iterator was
incomplete, abandoning the iterator's class body and dropping all its member
functions).  That failure propagated into the elaboration of `std::span<int> s(arr, 3)`
so that main was truncated right after the `int arr[]` declaration: the span
object, the constructor call and BOTH __CPROVER_assert calls were dropped.
`--show-properties` listed neither `span size` nor `span element`, so the old
CORE test (which only matched `^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$`) passed vacuously.

With the iterator's `iterator_concept` alias kept lazy ([iterator.concepts.general]
+ [temp.inst]/1-2: it is an inert concept-dispatch member that nothing depends on
structurally and a typedef-name may denote an incomplete type), the iterator's
member functions are instantiated, main is no longer truncated, and both
assertions are now genuinely generated.  They currently FAIL because std::span's
own (pointer, size_type) constructor is mis-instantiated in CBMC (its body comes
out empty, so _M_ptr/_M_extent are nondeterministic) -- a separate, genuine
front-end bug now exposed rather than hidden.

Reclassify CORE once std::span's constructor instantiates a correct body so the
populated span verifies genuinely.
